<article class="env-lemma" id="0B1P">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B1P">Lemma <span data-tag="0B1P">43.23.1</span>.</a> Let $V$ be a vector space of dimension $n + 1$. Let $X \subset \mathbf{P}(V)$ be a closed subscheme. If $X \not= \mathbf{P}(V)$, then there is a nonempty Zariski open $U \subset \mathbf{P}(V)$ such that for all closed points $p \in U$ the restriction of the projection $r_ p$ defines a finite morphism $r_ p|_ X : X \to \mathbf{P}(W_ p)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We claim the lemma holds with $U = \mathbf{P}(V) \setminus X$. For a closed point $p$ of $U$ we indeed obtain a morphism $r_ p|_ X : X \to \mathbf{P}(W_ p)$. This morphism is proper because $X$ is a proper scheme (Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/01WC" data-tag="01WC">29.44.5</a> and <a href="/tag/01W6" data-tag="01W6">29.42.7</a>). On the other hand, the fibres of $r_ p$ are affine lines as can be seen by a direct calculation. Hence the fibres of $r_ p|X$ are proper and affine, whence finite (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01WN" data-tag="01WN">29.45.11</a>). Finally, a proper morphism with finite fibres is finite (Cohomology of Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/02OG" data-tag="02OG">30.21.1</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>